    "result_text": "Done. The build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe `riskFlag` field on the owner response (`GET`/`POST /api/owners`), returning `true` when **any** of the three conditions hold, else `false`.\n\n**1. OpenAPI schema** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a `riskFlag` read-only boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the new field.\n\n**2. Computation** (`OwnerMapper.java`) \u2014 added a `riskFlag(owner)` mapping that ORs the three signals, reusing existing derived logic where it already exists:\n\n- **Possible duplicate** \u2192 `possibleDuplicate == true` (set on create by the controller's soft-match rule).\n- **City over soft capacity** \u2192 reuses the existing `capacityWarning(owner)` (the city is in the soft-cap warning window `[40, 50)`, i.e. over the soft threshold of 40 but below the hard cap of 50 where creates are rejected).\n- **Disposable-adjacent email** \u2192 a new `disposableAdjacentEmail(owner)` helper. Since the create endpoint already rejects *exact* disposable domains (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) with a 400, \"adjacent\" is defined as a domain sharing a second-level label with one of those providers \u2014 catching subdomains (`promo.mailinator.com`) and alternate TLDs (`tempmail.co`) that slip past the exact-match blocklist. Returns `false` when there's no email.\n\nThe provided test `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` \u2014 a normal structured owner with no email, a unique city, and no duplicate \u2014 correctly yields `riskFlag: false`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the one-line spec doesn't precisely define \"disposable-adjacent,\" and only the false-path case is covered by a visible test. I chose the shared-second-level-label interpretation because it's deterministic, reuses the existing blocklist as the single source of truth, and is consistent with the fact that exact disposable domains are already blocked at create time. If the intended definition is different (e.g. edit-distance/typosquat matching), that's the one place to adjust.",
